<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kertanegara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1268">1268</a>–<a href="/years/1292">1292</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Kertanagara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kertanegara was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Around c. 1275 he launched a major military expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, further weakening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a>. His defiance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> led to a Mongol expedition to Java, during which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> emerged.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kertanegara.jpg" alt="Kertanegara" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anandajoti Bhikkhu from Sadao, Thailand · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:063_Main_Statue,_Joko_Dolog_(39706345204).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kertanagara was the fifth ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a>, the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisnuwardhana">Wisnuwardhana</a>. The dynasty had come to power in 1222, when its first ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ken-arok">Ken Arok</a>, overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri Kingdom</a>. Kertanagara effectively held power from 1254 and formally succeeded on his father's death in 1268. He followed a mystical Tantric blend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, presented himself as a god-king embodying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and Buddha, and held many religious festivals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> reached the height of its power. He put down rebellions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> by Cayaraja in 1270 and Mahisha Rangkah in 1280, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> in 1284, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> and extended Javanese involvement in the spice trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamalayu-expedition">Pamalayu expedition</a> brought the Melayu Kingdom of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> under his control, and lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> offered their submission. When envoys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> arrived in 1289 to demand tribute, he had their faces branded and their ears cut and sent them back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and in 1292 Kublai ordered a punitive naval expedition.</p><p>Before it arrived, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakatwang">Jayakatwang</a>, prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a>'s strongest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, rebelled while most of the Javanese army was overseas. A diversionary attack drew the remaining troops north, and Jayakatwang struck the undefended capital from the mountainous south. Kertanagara was killed with many courtiers in his palace in May or June 1292.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a> escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a> and founded the settlement that grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>. Through his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gayatri-rajapatni">Gayatri Rajapatni</a>, Kertanagara became ancestor of the Rajasa dynasty that ruled it. In 1351 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a> issued an inscription for a funerary temple dedicated to him. Kertanagara had already named the idea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusantara">Nusantara</a>, a united archipelago, in the Mula Malurung inscription of 1255, and used the term Cakravala Mandala Dvipantara in 1275; historians suggest Gajah Mada took him as a model in his own aim of uniting the islands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kertanagara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Majapahit Empire was established in the late 13th century in eastern Java after the collapse of the Singhasari kingdom, whose last king, <b>Kertanegara</b>, was killed by the rebel Jayakatwang of Kediri in 1292.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Former dependencies broke away, and in 1275 <b>Kertanagara</b> of Singhasari launched the Pamalayu expedition against Malayu, which further weakened the Sumatran centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1275 <b>Kertanegara of Singhasari</b> sent the Pamalayu expedition, and in 1286 a statue of Amoghapasa was sent from Java to King Tribhuwanaraja Mauli Warmadewa, as the Padang Roco inscription records.</blockquote><a href="/state/dharmasraya">Dharmasraya</a></li><li><blockquote>Jayakatwang, ruler of Gelang-gelang and a descendant of the kings of the Kediri Kingdom, was a vassal of <b>Kertanegara of Singhasari</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gelang-gelang">Gelang-gelang</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition dates Sumenep's founding to 1269, when <b>Kertanegara of Singhasari</b> appointed Arya Wiraraja as its adipati.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sumenep">Kingdom of Sumenep</a></li><li><blockquote>Its last king <b>Kertanegara</b> pursued an ambitious overseas policy: the Pamalayu expedition of 1275 brought Sumatran Dharmasraya into his orbit, and he claimed influence over Bali and Madura.</blockquote><a href="/state/singhasari">Singhasari</a></li></ul></section>
